WEBVTT

00:00.300 --> 00:01.620
Lecturer: So let's start this section off

00:01.620 --> 00:05.400
with a nice and easy topic, The Git Docs.

00:05.400 --> 00:09.600
Yes, this is a very funny joke because these are pictures

00:09.600 --> 00:13.560
of doctors, but we're not actually talking about doctors.

00:13.560 --> 00:15.717
We're talking about the Git Documentation.

00:15.717 --> 00:17.550
Can you believe how funny that is?

00:17.550 --> 00:18.900
So dumb jokes aside,

00:18.900 --> 00:20.980
we're talking about the Git Documentation

00:22.001 --> 00:23.220
which you may want to reference at some point

00:23.220 --> 00:24.990
if you have questions or you wanna learn more

00:24.990 --> 00:27.660
about one of the commands that I'm showing you.

00:27.660 --> 00:32.583
So the docs are accessible at git-scm.com.

00:33.420 --> 00:34.680
I never remember that.

00:34.680 --> 00:38.400
I just google Git and then I end up on this webpage.

00:38.400 --> 00:40.500
And then we want to go to Documentation

00:40.500 --> 00:41.490
pretty much every time.

00:41.490 --> 00:42.840
That's where I go.

00:42.840 --> 00:44.910
And you know, I'm not going to About,

00:44.910 --> 00:47.370
I don't need to see the advantages of Git compared

00:47.370 --> 00:49.200
to something else, I'm already using it.

00:49.200 --> 00:50.820
I just want the docs.

00:50.820 --> 00:52.650
Now there are a couple of sections here,

00:52.650 --> 00:53.850
and most of the time

00:53.850 --> 00:55.770
I end up looking at the Reference Manual.

00:55.770 --> 00:59.430
Occasionally this book called Pro Git.

00:59.430 --> 01:01.290
Pretty much never the Videos.

01:01.290 --> 01:03.600
So the Reference Manual, we'll start there,

01:03.600 --> 01:04.800
is exactly what it says.

01:04.800 --> 01:07.560
It's a reference manual, it's all the Git commands

01:07.560 --> 01:09.750
and main topics.

01:09.750 --> 01:12.690
It is not ordered, it's not a step-by-step guide,

01:12.690 --> 01:14.280
it's just a reference.

01:14.280 --> 01:18.150
So for example, we've been using git-commit.

01:18.150 --> 01:20.520
If I wanna learn more about git-commit,

01:20.520 --> 01:22.710
I can click on this, I'll zoom in.

01:22.710 --> 01:24.510
It tells us roughly what it does,

01:24.510 --> 01:26.610
record changes to the repository.

01:26.610 --> 01:29.130
Here's a more detailed description.

01:29.130 --> 01:32.820
But then as I scroll down this is what I end up looking at

01:32.820 --> 01:36.000
occasionally on the docs, all of these options.

01:36.000 --> 01:38.460
So this is one command, git-commit,

01:38.460 --> 01:41.490
and we've used one option, which is -m.

01:41.490 --> 01:45.210
Could find that on here somewhere, -m,

01:45.210 --> 01:48.210
which is short for --message=&lt;msg>.

01:48.210 --> 01:51.180
Use the given &lt;msg> as the commit message.

01:51.180 --> 01:54.150
We already know that, hopefully, you know how to do that.

01:54.150 --> 01:56.700
But here is where we would find that information.

01:56.700 --> 01:58.530
So there are lots of options

01:58.530 --> 02:00.870
for every single one of the Git commands I've shown you,

02:00.870 --> 02:03.180
and it's honestly overwhelming.

02:03.180 --> 02:06.580
I use very few of them, but there are a lot available.

02:06.580 --> 02:08.520
So if you ever have questions about them,

02:08.520 --> 02:11.310
if you see somebody use a command, git-commit

02:11.310 --> 02:14.970
and then the -z flag and you have no idea what that is,

02:14.970 --> 02:16.770
you can read about it here.

02:16.770 --> 02:21.770
Also, if we keep scrolling down, we will find examples.

02:21.810 --> 02:23.970
So there are some examples that show, you know,

02:23.970 --> 02:26.880
how to use git-commit in this example.

02:26.880 --> 02:28.140
But on, you know, another page,

02:28.140 --> 02:29.370
if we're looking at git-log,

02:29.370 --> 02:31.110
there will be examples for git-log

02:31.110 --> 02:32.760
or for some of the more advanced commands,

02:32.760 --> 02:34.530
that can be helpful.

02:34.530 --> 02:35.850
And that's pretty much it.

02:35.850 --> 02:38.250
I mean there's Discussion down at the bottom.

02:38.250 --> 02:40.530
Basically for every Git command,

02:40.530 --> 02:42.870
we deal with this really thorough,

02:42.870 --> 02:46.980
but also intimidating set of documentation.

02:46.980 --> 02:51.210
So if you are looking for something more educational

02:51.210 --> 02:55.170
or more sort of teachy, you can go to the book.

02:55.170 --> 02:58.020
And this is grouped into topics that are ordered

02:58.020 --> 02:59.190
and it explains things.

02:59.190 --> 03:01.920
So for example, we haven't yet covered Branching,

03:01.920 --> 03:03.930
but if you wanted to learn more about Branching

03:03.930 --> 03:06.420
or if my explanation is insufficient

03:06.420 --> 03:07.800
once you get to that section,

03:07.800 --> 03:09.900
you can read more and have diagrams

03:09.900 --> 03:12.300
and sort of step-by-step explanation here.

03:12.300 --> 03:13.860
And it's pretty, well, I shouldn't say pretty,

03:13.860 --> 03:15.420
it's very well written.

03:15.420 --> 03:20.070
It's a well done book and it is a nice antidote to the dry

03:20.070 --> 03:24.930
and you know, purely reference manual documentation.

03:24.930 --> 03:28.920
The book really does have a clear path through it.

03:28.920 --> 03:31.770
So I don't necessarily recommend reading the entire book.

03:31.770 --> 03:33.930
But I mean, if I come here occasionally

03:33.930 --> 03:35.190
when I have questions about things,

03:35.190 --> 03:37.830
some commands that I haven't used in a while

03:37.830 --> 03:39.870
or configuring things,

03:39.870 --> 03:42.540
the book is very helpful to explain them.

03:42.540 --> 03:44.130
And then if you just have a one-off question

03:44.130 --> 03:45.270
about what is that flag

03:45.270 --> 03:48.900
or is there a way to do X with the git-log command,

03:48.900 --> 03:51.270
you can go read about it on the Reference Manual.

03:51.270 --> 03:55.200
Just do your best to stay sane while reading these docs.

03:55.200 --> 03:56.250
There's so many options,

03:56.250 --> 03:58.650
so many flags for every single command.

03:58.650 --> 04:00.810
All right, so that's how we get to the docs.

04:00.810 --> 04:05.810
Git-scm.com, Documentation, Reference Manual or Book.
